\description{
Power analysis for exploratory factor analysis, in two modes selected by \code{mode}.

\code{mode = "rmsea"} (the default) gives the analytic statistical power of the root
mean square error of approximation (RMSEA) tests of close and not-close fit
(MacCallum, Browne, & Sugawara, 1996): given the model degrees of freedom and a
sample size it returns the power to reject the null hypothesis, and given a target
power instead of a sample size it returns the sample size required to reach it.

\code{mode = "simulation"} runs a Monte-Carlo study of a known population: it draws
\code{n_datasets} samples of size \code{N} (via \code{\link[=efa_simulate]{efa_simulate()}}) and, over the replicates,
reports how often each factor-retention criterion recovers the true number of
factors (the \emph{hit-rate}), how often the fitted loadings recover the population
structure (\emph{structure recovery}, by Tucker congruence), and the convergence and
Heywood-case rate of the fit.
}
\section{RMSEA mode}{
The RMSEA fit statistic is referred to a noncentral chi-square distribution.
For a model with \code{df} degrees of freedom and population RMSEA \eqn{\varepsilon},
the noncentrality parameter is
\deqn{\lambda = (N - 1)\, df\, \varepsilon^2 / G,}
where \eqn{N} is the sample size and \eqn{G} the number of groups (\code{group}).

Two tests are supported, selected by \code{type} (never by the ordering of \code{eps0}
and \code{eps1}):
\describe{
\item{\code{"close"}}{The test of \emph{close fit} (MacCallum et al., 1996): the null
hypothesis is that the fit is close (RMSEA \eqn{\le} \code{eps0}, conventionally
0.05), and power is computed against a worse alternative (\code{eps1},
conventionally 0.08, so \code{eps0 < eps1}). The critical value is the upper
\code{alpha} point of the null distribution
(\code{qchisq(alpha, df, ncp0, lower.tail = FALSE)}) and the power is the mass of
the alternative distribution beyond it.}
\item{\code{"notclose"}}{The test of \emph{not-close fit}: the null hypothesis is that
the fit is not close (RMSEA \eqn{\ge} \code{eps0}), and power is computed against
a better alternative (\code{eps1}, conventionally 0.01, so \code{eps0 > eps1}). Here
the rejection region is the lower tail, so the critical value is the lower
\code{alpha} point of the null distribution and the power is the mass of the
alternative distribution below it.}
}
When \code{eps0} and \code{eps1} are ordered the wrong way round for the chosen \code{type} a
message is emitted but the requested test is still computed. Equal \code{eps0} and
\code{eps1} leave nothing to detect and are an error.

Power increases monotonically with \code{N}, so the required sample size (the
smallest \code{N} reaching \code{power}) is found by bisection. It is a per-group sample
size: with \code{group > 1} the noncentrality carries the \code{1 / group} factor above.
}

\section{Simulation mode}{
The population is supplied either as a factor model (\code{Lambda}, with optional \code{Phi}
and \code{Psi}) or as a ready correlation matrix \code{R}, and passed to \code{\link[=efa_simulate]{efa_simulate()}},
which draws \code{n_datasets} samples of size \code{N}. By default the population fits the
factor model exactly, which overstates how well the criteria and the fit recover
the structure; supplying a misfit target (\code{target_rmsea} and/or \code{target_cfi})
perturbs it with model error (\code{model_error}, \code{"TKL"} by default) so the model fits
only approximately -- a more realistic target (MacCallum, 2003). The true number
of factors \code{k_true} is \code{ncol(Lambda)} for a factor-model population, or \code{k} for a
bare \code{R}.

Each replicate is analysed three ways. \strong{Hit-rate}: every criterion in \code{criteria}
(drawn from the same registry as \code{\link[=N_FACTORS]{N_FACTORS()}}) is run and its suggested number of
factors compared with \code{k_true}; the hit-rate is the proportion of agreements over the
replicates on which the criterion returned a definite factor count (replicates where
it errored or was undecided are excluded, not counted as misses). \strong{Structure
recovery} (only for a factor-model population): the \code{k_true}-factor model is fitted
with \code{\link[=EFA]{EFA()}}, the rotated loadings are matched to the population loadings (column
permutation and sign), and the matched-factor Tucker congruences (Lorenzo-Seva & ten
Berge, 2006) are compared with \code{recovery_threshold}; recovery counts a replicate a
success when the smallest (\code{min}) or the average (\code{mean}) matched congruence reaches
the threshold. \strong{Convergence}: the same fit supplies the proportion of replicates
whose fit completed and, among the completed fits, the proportion that converged and
that produced a Heywood case.

Replicates are analysed in parallel with \pkg{future.apply}; a plan is selected with
\code{\link[future:plan]{future::plan()}}. Each replicate is bound to its own reproducible random-number
stream, so with a fixed \code{seed} the result is identical regardless of the number of
workers, and the caller's random-number state is left unchanged.
}

\examples{
# Power of the test of close fit at N = 200 for a 100-df model
efa_power(df = 100, N = 200)

# Deriving df from the model dimensions instead of giving it directly
efa_power(p = 20, k = 3, N = 200)

# Required (per-group) sample size for 80\% power
efa_power(df = 100, power = 0.80)

# Test of not-close fit
efa_power(df = 100, N = 200, type = "notclose")

\donttest{
# Simulation mode: retention hit-rate and structure recovery for a known
# three-factor population at N = 300
efa_power("simulation", Lambda = population_models$loadings$baseline,
          Phi = population_models$phis_3$moderate, N = 300,
          n_datasets = 50, criteria = c("EKC", "MAP"), seed = 42)
}

}
